@startuml
title Agent Teams Workflow v2 — capability grant versus invocation
skinparam backgroundColor #FFFAF8
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam ArrowColor #553C35
skinparam packageStyle rectangle

actor "Author wallet" as author #FFD4BA
actor "Invoker wallet" as invoker #FFD4BA

package "Public designators" #E2F1FA {
  component "rho:id: URI" as uri #CDE7F5
  component "F1r3sky atproto record" as post #CDE7F5
}
package "Embers trust boundary" #FFF0DB {
  component "Rholang compiler" as compiler #FFD5AC
  component "insertSigned wrapper" as wrapper #FFD5AC
}
package "Capability boundary — unforgeable names" #E7F3E7 {
  component "Team name: bundle+ over\nmanifest / guard / activity / entry" as teamcap #B9DCBE
  component "Per-run fresh channels" as runchans #B9DCBE
}

author --> compiler : signed definition (wallet key)
compiler --> teamcap : new-bind name, install four clauses
teamcap --> wrapper : bundle+ (send-only) export
wrapper --> uri : insertSigned\n<latex>\mathrm{sig} = \mathrm{blake2b256}(timestamp, deployerPubKey, version)</latex>\nversion compare-and-set
uri --> post : publish (URI becomes public)

invoker --> uri : unauthenticated lookup
uri --> teamcap : knowledge of the URI = authority to send
teamcap --> runchans : entry clause mints per-run channels
runchans --> invoker : beginRun records the INVOKER as owner

note right of wrapper #FFF6C9
GRANT is a signature over the publication tuple:
only the registry key may replace the entry.
INVOKE is possession of the URI: lookup is
unauthenticated, so the designator is public
while send-authority lives in the bundled name.
end note
note bottom of post #FAD8D8
Publishing hands the entry capability and the
manifest read to everyone. It confers no
authority over the publisher's own runs.
end note
@enduml
